WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:02.752
Where there's life,
there's bound to be music.

00:00:02.836 --> 00:00:04.963
That's not to say that
one causes the other,

00:00:05.046 --> 00:00:07.424
but there's a reason
that silent forests

00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:09.384
are a spooky portent of doom.

00:00:09.467 --> 00:00:10.635
In many mythologies,

00:00:10.719 --> 00:00:13.179
music is associated with
the explosion of life

00:00:13.263 --> 00:00:14.806
and bursts of creativity.

00:00:14.889 --> 00:00:18.643
But it's also used to explore
the dark side of human nature,

00:00:18.727 --> 00:00:20.937
the desire to manipulate
our surroundings,

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.731
or to control others.

00:00:22.814 --> 00:00:25.066
Every time we play
a musical instrument,

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:26.735
be it a cosmic conch shell,

00:00:26.818 --> 00:00:28.653
an emotionally
intelligent harp,

00:00:28.737 --> 00:00:30.488
or a heartbreaking lyre,

00:00:30.572 --> 00:00:32.907
we are creating something
beautiful and mysterious,

00:00:32.991 --> 00:00:36.327
but we're also
manifesting our will.

00:00:36.411 --> 00:00:39.664
This tension is evident in the
stories we tell about music,

00:00:39.748 --> 00:00:41.082
the instruments that make it,

00:00:41.166 --> 00:00:42.917
and the people that perfect it.

00:00:43.001 --> 00:00:45.670
[upbeat music]

00:00:46.963 --> 00:00:49.466
In some cultures, the sound
of music is associated

00:00:49.549 --> 00:00:51.551
with the blooming
of life itself.

00:00:51.634 --> 00:00:54.554
Hindu cosmology depicts one
of the principle deities,

00:00:54.637 --> 00:00:58.224
Vishnu, holding a shanka,
or conch shell.

00:00:58.308 --> 00:01:00.643
During the Churning
of the Cosmic Ocean,

00:01:00.727 --> 00:01:01.936
Vishnu knew blew
the conch shell

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.355
to sound the first
noise of creation,

00:01:04.439 --> 00:01:07.484
an "ommm" that
continues to reverberate

00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:08.985
throughout the universe.

00:01:09.068 --> 00:01:12.405
With its concentric spirals
and deep echoing sound,

00:01:12.489 --> 00:01:14.949
the shanka is
a material manifestation

00:01:15.033 --> 00:01:17.035
of eternal cosmic energy.

00:01:17.118 --> 00:01:19.579
Other creation myths reveal
the importance of music

00:01:19.662 --> 00:01:21.331
to humans from the beginning.

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:24.375
Sumerian myth offers us
the tale of Ninmah

00:01:24.459 --> 00:01:27.504
the primeval mother goddess
who shaped the first humans

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:29.589
from fresh underground water.

00:01:29.672 --> 00:01:32.300
Her son Enki, crowned
the second ever human

00:01:32.383 --> 00:01:33.593
to grace the earth,

00:01:33.676 --> 00:01:36.513
a master of the musical
arts and a chieftain,

00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:39.182
indicating both
the sacred nature of music

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:41.476
and its centrality
to human nature.

00:01:41.559 --> 00:01:43.978
Exploring the links
between life, music,

00:01:44.062 --> 00:01:46.689
and creativity is
a common mythical theme.

00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:49.567
In Norse mythology,
Bragi, son of Odin,

00:01:49.651 --> 00:01:52.654
was given a magical harp
and sent on his way to Asgard

00:01:52.737 --> 00:01:55.406
from his mother's home
in the Land of Giants.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:59.035
With his harp and the magical
runes carved on his tongue,

00:01:59.118 --> 00:02:01.663
Bragi's music had the power
to bring plants to life,

00:02:01.746 --> 00:02:05.166
control animals,
and ward off harmful spells.

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:08.628
In these stories, music doesn't
just play in the background.

00:02:08.711 --> 00:02:12.465
Instead, it actively has
the power to awaken the world.

00:02:12.549 --> 00:02:16.135
From epic cosmic ripples to
the most delicate flower.

00:02:16.219 --> 00:02:18.388
If music has these epic powers,

00:02:18.471 --> 00:02:21.224
it's no surprise that mythical
characters have used it

00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:23.434
to exert their will
on the world.

00:02:23.518 --> 00:02:26.521
The leader of the Tuatha De
Danann from Irish mythology

00:02:26.604 --> 00:02:29.065
was Dagda, a figure of plenty.

00:02:29.149 --> 00:02:31.818
His cauldron was always
bubbling with stew,

00:02:31.901 --> 00:02:33.736
his pig was always growing,

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:37.740
and his magical club had the
power to either wound or heal.

00:02:37.824 --> 00:02:40.118
But perhaps his most
powerful instrument

00:02:40.201 --> 00:02:44.414
was his four-angled harp,
also known as the Uaithne.

00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:48.626
Also called the Coir Ceathar
Chuir or Four Angled Music,

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:51.296
the harp could put the
seasons in the correct order

00:02:51.379 --> 00:02:53.631
and control human emotions.

00:02:53.715 --> 00:02:54.674
No big deal.

00:02:54.757 --> 00:02:56.676
The Uaithne was
coveted for its powers

00:02:56.759 --> 00:02:58.678
by the fairy King Brush.

00:02:58.761 --> 00:03:01.055
But when he got his
thieving hands on it,

00:03:01.139 --> 00:03:02.599
the harp fell silent.

00:03:02.682 --> 00:03:05.143
For no one but the Dagda
could make it sing.

00:03:05.226 --> 00:03:07.687
When Dagda uttered
a charm from afar,

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:09.939
the harp came to life and
rushed toward its master

00:03:10.023 --> 00:03:13.151
killing nine of the
king's men on route.

00:03:13.234 --> 00:03:16.279
Another tale of music's
ability to control comes to us

00:03:16.362 --> 00:03:18.197
in the legend of the Owl Husband

00:03:18.281 --> 00:03:21.492
from the Passamaquoddy people
in the northeastern U.S.

00:03:21.576 --> 00:03:24.871
The story tells of a young
woman who refused to marry.

00:03:24.954 --> 00:03:27.415
Her father decreed that
whoever made the embers

00:03:27.498 --> 00:03:30.084
of a fading fire
blaze with new flames

00:03:30.168 --> 00:03:31.085
could marry his daughter.

00:03:31.169 --> 00:03:34.547
Of course, no one could
reverse the fires,

00:03:34.631 --> 00:03:36.758
except for the wily horned owl

00:03:36.841 --> 00:03:39.135
who disguised himself
as a handsome hunter

00:03:39.218 --> 00:03:42.180
and used his magical powers
to reignite the fire.

00:03:42.263 --> 00:03:44.474
It was only after their
first night together

00:03:44.557 --> 00:03:46.601
that the woman perceived
his yellow eyes

00:03:46.684 --> 00:03:48.102
and pointed ears.

00:03:48.186 --> 00:03:50.313
Horrified, she fled.

00:03:50.396 --> 00:03:52.231
The owl tried to
trick her again,

00:03:52.315 --> 00:03:54.233
coming to her in
a different disguise,

00:03:54.317 --> 00:03:56.444
but she was able to
perceive his true nature

00:03:56.527 --> 00:03:58.905
and she alluded him
for a second time.

00:03:58.988 --> 00:04:02.283
Furious, he consulted
his sorcerer owl aunt

00:04:02.367 --> 00:04:04.160
who fashioned a magic flute
with the power

00:04:04.243 --> 00:04:07.997
to send anyone who heard
its tones into a love trance.

00:04:08.081 --> 00:04:10.792
At the sound of sweet music
coming from the forest,

00:04:10.875 --> 00:04:12.961
the young woman was enchanted.

00:04:13.044 --> 00:04:15.672
By the time she found
herself deep in the forest

00:04:15.755 --> 00:04:17.173
searching for her love,

00:04:17.257 --> 00:04:18.967
the horned owl swooped down

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:21.177
and snatched
her up in his talons.

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:24.222
So music in mythology
is usually beautiful,

00:04:24.305 --> 00:04:26.766
but it often holds
the power to manipulate.

00:04:26.849 --> 00:04:28.935
In the harp that toys
with human emotions

00:04:29.018 --> 00:04:32.271
or the flute that entrances
unsuspecting mortals,

00:04:32.355 --> 00:04:35.650
music is seen as a mysterious
and overwhelming force

00:04:35.733 --> 00:04:37.568
that's not to be taken lightly.

00:04:37.652 --> 00:04:40.488
This complex power is
further explored in myths

00:04:40.571 --> 00:04:43.574
that examine music as a portal
to the supernatural world.

00:04:43.658 --> 00:04:44.617
In Finnish mythology,

00:04:44.701 --> 00:04:47.537
the demigod Vainamoinen
plucked fish bones

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:50.957
from the base of a waterfall
to fashion a magical harp.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:54.293
Only he could control
its joy-bringing powers

00:04:54.377 --> 00:04:57.005
until the sea god Ahto
stole it for himself.

00:04:57.088 --> 00:04:59.924
Stung, Vainamoinen
wove a second harp

00:05:00.008 --> 00:05:02.969
from birchwood and
a maiden's golden locks.

00:05:03.052 --> 00:05:06.014
He took his new harp with
him deep into the underworld

00:05:06.097 --> 00:05:07.849
on a hunt for the Sampo,

00:05:07.932 --> 00:05:10.310
a magical device that
could bring good fortune.

00:05:10.393 --> 00:05:13.438
With the help of the harp,
Vainamoinen found the Sampo

00:05:13.521 --> 00:05:16.399
and lulled the inhabitants
of the underworld to sleep.

00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:20.069
Only for them to awaken and
overtake him at the last minute.

00:05:20.153 --> 00:05:22.989
Eventually, the Sampo
was lost forever.

00:05:23.072 --> 00:05:25.616
Like Vainamoinen,
the Greek hero Orpheus

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:28.745
could only wield his music
against supernatural forces

00:05:28.828 --> 00:05:30.329
to a point.

00:05:30.413 --> 00:05:33.541
Orpheus was known as the
most masterful musician

00:05:33.624 --> 00:05:36.919
who perfected the lyre and
sang stunning love poems.

00:05:37.003 --> 00:05:40.339
When his beloved wife
Eurydice died by viper attack,

00:05:40.423 --> 00:05:43.926
Orpheus ventured into
the underworld to
beg for her return.

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:45.344
With his haunting lyre,

00:05:45.428 --> 00:05:47.638
he played sweet
and mournful music

00:05:47.722 --> 00:05:49.515
to tame the
three-headed guardian

00:05:49.599 --> 00:05:51.100
of the underworld Cerberus,

00:05:51.184 --> 00:05:54.604
and softened the hearts of
gods Hades and Persephone.

00:05:54.687 --> 00:05:57.106
The gods of the underworld
agreed to let Eurydice

00:05:57.190 --> 00:05:59.609
follow Orpheus back to
the land of the living

00:05:59.692 --> 00:06:02.737
on the condition that
Orpheus did not look back

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:04.447
to check that she
was following him.

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:05.990
Just as they neared the surface,

00:06:06.074 --> 00:06:09.285
Orpheus was overcome
with anxious curiosity.

00:06:09.368 --> 00:06:12.747
He turned around only to
see Eurydice falling back

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:13.998
into the clutches of Hell.

00:06:14.082 --> 00:06:15.249
He was so distraught

00:06:15.333 --> 00:06:17.376
that he spent the
rest of his life alone

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:19.462
singing about broken hearts.

00:06:19.545 --> 00:06:22.465
Until a volatile group of
women, known as the Maenads,

00:06:22.548 --> 00:06:24.926
ripped him to shreds
for his lack of cheer.

00:06:25.009 --> 00:06:27.929
Orpheus' severed head
carried on singing in death

00:06:28.012 --> 00:06:30.723
and so his music
continued to resound.

00:06:30.807 --> 00:06:33.142
Here, music is the key
to the underworld,

00:06:33.226 --> 00:06:35.853
but it also transcends
tragic circumstances

00:06:35.937 --> 00:06:38.981
and retains its mysterious
power beyond the grave.

00:06:39.065 --> 00:06:42.318
Orpheus's singing head is not
the only ancient instrument

00:06:42.401 --> 00:06:44.362
with supernatural associations.

00:06:44.445 --> 00:06:45.738
In Chinese mythology,

00:06:45.822 --> 00:06:48.699
Lin Lun was believed to
be the creator of music

00:06:48.783 --> 00:06:50.618
who invented the
ancient bamboo flute

00:06:50.701 --> 00:06:53.788
and tuned it to mimic
the sound of the phoenix.

00:06:53.871 --> 00:06:56.582
To the Nahuas people who
lived in what is now Mexico,

00:06:56.666 --> 00:06:58.626
the Death Whistle signaled...

00:06:58.709 --> 00:07:00.002
[Death Whistle screaming]

00:07:00.086 --> 00:07:02.004
you guessed it, that
someone was going to die.

00:07:02.088 --> 00:07:03.756
It was constructed from stone

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:07.593
and carved with symbols of
death like skulls and owls.

00:07:07.677 --> 00:07:09.637
While its exact
uses are not known,

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:12.390
scholars have suggested
that their players use them

00:07:12.473 --> 00:07:14.934
to echo the howling
wind of the spirit world

00:07:15.017 --> 00:07:17.687
in funereal or
sacrificial rituals.

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:18.771
In these examples,

00:07:18.855 --> 00:07:20.898
musical instruments
mediate the relationship

00:07:20.982 --> 00:07:24.735
between human, natural,
and spiritual worlds.

00:07:24.819 --> 00:07:26.988
Making these instruments
from natural materials

00:07:27.071 --> 00:07:29.740
and training the body
to play them are acts

00:07:29.824 --> 00:07:31.993
that are grounded in
the physical world.

00:07:32.076 --> 00:07:33.619
But when played,

00:07:33.703 --> 00:07:36.956
these instruments evoke
something intangible
and mystifying.

00:07:37.039 --> 00:07:38.583
Across mythology,

00:07:38.666 --> 00:07:40.877
stories about music often
come down to the question

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:42.962
of what we can
and can't control.

00:07:43.045 --> 00:07:46.257
Tales of music nudging
nature into bloom,

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:47.300
exploiting human emotion,

00:07:47.383 --> 00:07:50.720
or communicating
with divine entities,

00:07:50.803 --> 00:07:51.846
we see characters trying

00:07:51.929 --> 00:07:55.016
to direct the world's
most enigmatic forces.

00:07:55.099 --> 00:07:56.976
But we also glimpse
the power of music

00:07:57.059 --> 00:08:00.730
to change our perception and
transport us to other realms.

00:08:00.813 --> 00:08:03.149
Whether it's being
used for good or evil,

00:08:03.232 --> 00:08:06.027
music always retains
a kernel of mystery,

00:08:06.110 --> 00:08:08.779
one that often compels us
to surrender our reason

00:08:08.863 --> 00:08:11.699
and, hopefully, enjoy the vibes.

00:08:11.782 --> 00:08:14.410
Before you go, we want to
invite you to participate

00:08:14.493 --> 00:08:17.830
in PBS Digital Studio's
Annual Audience Survey.

00:08:17.914 --> 00:08:20.833
It's your chance to tell
PBS what you like to watch

00:08:20.917 --> 00:08:23.044
and even vote on new show ideas.

00:08:23.127 --> 00:08:25.421
In fact, "Fate and Fabled"
was voted on

00:08:25.504 --> 00:08:28.132
in a previous survey,
and here it is today.

00:08:28.216 --> 00:08:31.093
So we'd like to thank you for
making this show possible.

00:08:31.177 --> 00:08:34.305
And if you'd like to continue
shaping the future of PBS,

00:08:34.388 --> 00:08:36.349
there's a link in
the description below.

00:08:36.432 --> 00:08:37.683
Thanks!

